#. §0: `linear₂`
#. §1: `D → D N`
#: Game.Levels.Demo.L02_Overgeneration
msgid "The proof went through, and that is the problem. *a a the this dog* is not an expression of\n"
"English, yet §0 derives it, so the rule is too strong. Rule 4 takes an N and returns an N,\n"
"so it can apply again and again.\n"
"\n"
"Suppose instead we keep only Rule 3, §1, and set the goal to D. Then *this dog* becomes a D,\n"
"no rule applies after that, and ⟨a a the this dog, D⟩ cannot be derived. That is the result we want.\n"
"Failing to derive is also a good result: a good model should not allow more than the natural\n"
"language does."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `rule \"NAME\" x y …`
#. §1: `the_cat_DP`
#. §2: `\"D' -> D0 NP\"`
#. §3: `\"D′ → D⁰ NP\"`
#. §4: `as NAME`
#: Game.Metadata
msgid "§0 applies the rule NAME to the expressions x, y, … in\n"
"your hypotheses. The rule computes the result, which appears as a new\n"
"hypothesis with a readable name (for example §1). If the rule is not\n"
"defined on those inputs, it is rejected.\n"
"\n"
"Rule names may be typed with ASCII: §2 means §3.\n"
"Add §4 at the end to choose the name of the result yourself."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.Demo.L04_MP
msgid "Look at the last step. Before Internal MERGE, {DP, vP} had no label: neither D nor v\n"
"shares a feature with the other. After it, the lower DP is a copy, the labeling algorithm\n"
"sees only vP, and the new root {DP, TP} is labeled ⟨φ,φ⟩ by shared φ.\n"
"Externalization pronounced only the higher copy: *the cat will sleep*.\n"
"\n"
"Three levels, three generations of generative syntax, one language.\n"
"In each, a grammar is categories, a lexicon and rules — data — and Lean checks every step."
msgstr ""

#: Game
msgid "This demo shows one formal language expressing grammars from three eras of generative syntax:\n"
"\n"
"1. a linear grammar,\n"
"2. X-bar theory with GB modules (c-selection, Case, EPP, Infl lowering),\n"
"3. the Minimalist Program of the workspace era (MERGE on workspaces, labeling).\n"
"\n"
"The language is Bare Grammar (Keenan & Stabler 2003), implemented in Lean 4. A grammar is\n"
"data — categories, a lexicon, rules — and an expression belongs to the language when there is\n"
"a derivation of it. Every step you take is checked by Lean's kernel."
msgstr ""

