msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.Demo.L03_XBarGB
msgid "Twelve applications of four schema rules, all with the command from levels 1 and 2.\n"
"\n"
"GB's modules entered as conditions on the domains of the schema rules — which complement a\n"
"head selects, where a DP gets Case, which head needs a specifier, which rule lowers the\n"
"affix — not as new machinery in the language."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `?`
#. §1: `copy(…)`
#. §2: `select the n cat will v sleep`
#. §3: `merge P Q`
#. §4: `the`
#. §5: `cat`
#. §6: `DP`
#. §7: `vP`
#. §8: `TP`
#. §9: `{DP, vP}`
#: Game.Levels.Demo.L04_MP
msgid "Same language, a grammar from a later era. Here **an expression is a workspace**.\n"
"Following Chomsky (2020, *UCLA Lectures*):\n"
"\n"
"  MERGE(P, Q, WS) = [{P, Q}, …] = WS′\n"
"\n"
"MERGE replaces P and Q by the set {P, Q}. If P and Q are both members of WS, this is\n"
"External MERGE, and WS loses one member. If P is a term of Q, it is Internal MERGE,\n"
"and WS keeps its size. One operation, not two.\n"
"\n"
"In Bare Grammar terms: the lexicon has one expression, the empty workspace.\n"
"Select and MERGE are rules whose domain is a decidable condition rather than a list\n"
"of categories.\n"
"\n"
"MERGE neither labels nor linearizes. Labels come from a labeling algorithm (after Chomsky 2013):\n"
"\n"
"- {H, XP}: H labels.\n"
"- {n, √root}, {v, √root}: a root cannot label, so n or v does.\n"
"- {XP, YP}: labeled ⟨φ,φ⟩ if both heads carry φ; otherwise no label —\n"
"  unless one of the two is a lower copy, which the algorithm does not see.\n"
"\n"
"Unlabeled nodes show as §0, lower copies as §1.\n"
"\n"
"**Goal:** a workspace with one object, every node labeled. Externalization — word order,\n"
"which copy is pronounced — is checked automatically. You only do narrow syntax.\n"
"\n"
"**Commands:**\n"
"\n"
"- §2 draws lexical items into WS.\n"
"- §3, where P and Q are lexical items (§4, §5), phrases named by their label\n"
"  (§6, §7, §8), or a set of two such names (§9).\n"
"\n"
"*Simplifications:* two occurrences of the same object count as copies;\n"
"Agree is not modeled (T's φ counts as valued); no phases."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.Demo.L01_Linear
msgid "Three rule applications. The same command — choose a rule, give it its inputs — runs every level.\n"
"What changes from level to level is only the grammar: its categories, its lexicon, its rules."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.Demo.L02_Overgeneration
msgid "A grammar that says too much"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `linear₁`
#. §1: `A → Adv A`
#. §2: `N → A N`
#. §3: `D → D N`
#. §4: `rule \"A -> Adv A\" very good`
#. §5: `very`
#. §6: `good`
#. §7: `very_good_A`
#: Game.Levels.Demo.L01_Linear
msgid "In Bare Grammar (Keenan & Stabler 2003), a grammar is data:\n"
"\n"
"- a set of **categories**,\n"
"- a **lexicon** of expressions, where an expression is a pair ⟨string, category⟩,\n"
"- a set of **rules**, each a partial function from expressions to an expression.\n"
"\n"
"The language L(G) is everything that can be built from the lexicon with the rules.\n"
"\n"
"The grammar of this level is §0:\n"
"\n"
"- Lexicon: a : D, very : Adv, good : A, day : N\n"
"- Rule 1: §1\n"
"- Rule 2: §2\n"
"- Rule 3: §3\n"
"\n"
"**Goal:** show that ⟨a very good day, D⟩ ∈ L(linear₁).\n"
"\n"
"**Command:** §4 applies Rule 1 to the expressions §5 and §6.\n"
"The rule computes the result and names it (§7); you only choose the rule and its inputs."
msgstr ""

